  <entry xml:base="https://kagi.com/api/v1/smallweb/feed">
    <title type="text">The Wisdom Paradox</title>
    <id>https://sketchplanations.com/the-wisdom-paradox</id>
    <updated>2026-08-09T11:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <published>2026-08-09T11:00:00+00:00</published>
    <link href="https://sketchplanations.com/the-wisdom-paradox" />
    <summary type="html">&lt;img src=&#34;https://images.prismic.io/sketchplanations/gLZDehKK6HI6UcSh_SP989-TheWisdomParadox.png?auto=format,compress&amp;amp;w=798&#34; /&gt;&lt;p&gt;Wisdom is a strange thing.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Wisdom Paradox is this: when we realise we are less wise than we thought, we become wiser.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Anthony de Mello, in his book &lt;a href=&#34;https://geni.us/one-minute-wisdom&#34; target=&#34;_self&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;One-Minute Wisdom&lt;/a&gt;, relates a master’s thoughts on wisdom:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;It always pleased the Master to hear people recognize their ignorance. “Wisdom tends to grow in proportion to one’s awareness of one’s ignorance,” he claimed.&lt;br /&gt;When asked for an explanation, he said, “When you come to see you are not as wise today as you thought you were yesterday, you are wiser today.”&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Or as the popular wisdom goes:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Good judgment comes from experience, and experience comes from bad judgment.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The naivety of youth is a lovely thing. As a teenager, you can be sure that the grown-ups are doing it all wrong, and the path to better is obvious. Then, as an adult, it somehow seems more difficult than before.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;My experience is that most things are harder than I thought they would be. Perhaps after getting something to work, I&amp;#39;m wiser than I was before I started, when I thought it would be easy. But I keep making that mistake, which perhaps shows how slowly wisdom arrives.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;That which seems like a false step is just the next step.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;— Agnes Martin&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The fool doth think he is wise, but the wise man knows himself to be a fool.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;— William Shakespeare&lt;br /&gt;(&lt;em&gt;As You Like It&lt;/em&gt;, Act 5, Scene 1)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A step backwards after making a wrong turn is a step in the right direction.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;— Kurt Vonnegut&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Related Ideas to the Wisdom Paradox&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Also see:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;The Dunning-Kruger Effect&lt;br /&gt;&lt;em&gt;I did this sketch for &lt;a href=&#34;https://sketchplanations.com/big-ideas-little-pictures&#34; target=&#34;_self&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;Big Ideas Little Pictures&lt;/a&gt;, but I ran out of space to include it (and am not allowed to post it online yet). Send me proof of purchase, and I’ll send it to you.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://sketchplanations.com/unknown-unknowns&#34; target=&#34;_self&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;Unknown Unknowns&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://sketchplanations.com/in-theory&#34; target=&#34;_self&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;In Theory, Practise is the Same as Theory&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://sketchplanations.com/dikw&#34; target=&#34;_self&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;Data Information Knowledge Wisdom&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://sketchplanations.com/physics-envy&#34; target=&#34;_self&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;Physics envy&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://sketchplanations.com/johari-window&#34; target=&#34;_self&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;The Johari Window&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://sketchplanations.com/the-lucretius-problem&#34; target=&#34;_self&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;The Lucretius Problem&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://sketchplanations.com/stages-of-competence-framework&#34; target=&#34;_self&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;The Consciousness-Competence Framework&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://sketchplanations.com/optimism-bias&#34; target=&#34;_self&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;Optimism Bias&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;https://sketchplanations.com/hofstadters-law&#34; target=&#34;_self&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;Hofstadter’s Law&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;The judgment and experience quote also has its own sketch in &lt;a href=&#34;https://sketchplanations.com/big-ideas-little-pictures&#34; target=&#34;_self&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;Big Ideas Little Pictures,&lt;/a&gt; p.126&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;</summary>
  </entry>
